## Why This Is Asked
Examines a practical sleep-wake path and basic data-structuring under concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Timer wheel with fixed slots for constant-time operations
- Safe concurrent enqueue and wakeup via spinlocks
- Tick progression and wrap-around handling

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct Thread { int id; struct Thread *next; } Thread;

#define WHEEL_SIZE 64
typedef struct {
  Thread *buckets[WHEEL_SIZE];
  int current_tick;
  SpinLock lock;
} TimerWheel;

void sleep_ms(Thread *t, int ms, TimerWheel *w) {
  int slot = (w->current_tick + ms) % WHEEL_SIZE;
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  t->next = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = t;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
}

void timer_tick(TimerWheel *w) {
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  int slot = w->current_tick % WHEEL_SIZE;
  Thread *cur = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = NULL;
  w->current_tick++;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
  while (cur) { wake(cur); cur = cur->next; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for correctness under concurrent enqueues and wakes?
- What changes if ms can exceed wheel size multiple times?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical virtual memory implementation skills including COW semantics, page table management, reference counting, synchronization, and TLB consistency. It evaluates the ability to handle concurrent page faults without memory leaks or race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Copy-On-Write (COW) optimization
- Page table updates and TLB invalidation
- Race condition handling and synchronization primitives
- Concurrent memory management

## Code Example
```c
void handle_write_fault(PTE *pte) {
  acquire(pte->lock);
  if (pte->refcount > 1) {
    Page *newpage = alloc_page();
    memcpy(newpage->addr, pte->frame->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    pte->frame = newpage;
    pte->writable = 1;
    pte->refcount--;
    invalidate_tlb(pte->vaddr);
  } else {
    pte->writable = 1;
  }
  release(pte->lock);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of user-space vs kernel-space concept interplay, synchronization primitives, and designing a lazy I/O cache with proper wakeups.

## Key Concepts

- Per-block state machine (EMPTY, PENDING, LOADED, ERROR)
- Fast path vs slow path with contention handling
- Wait queues (futex/condvar) and wakeup semantics
- Avoiding thundering herd with CAS and double-checked locking
- Error propagation from I/O to callers

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  void *data;
  atomic_int state; // 0=EMPTY,1=PENDING,2=LOADED,3=ERROR
  int waiters;
  pthread_mutex_t mutex;
  pthread_cond_t cond;
} cache_entry;

#define EMPTY 0
#define PENDING 1
#define LOADED 2
#define ERROR 3

void* block_read(int block_id) {
  cache_entry *entry = &cache[block_id];

  // Fast path
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    return entry->data;
  }

  pthread_mutex_lock(&entry->mutex);

  // Double-check after acquiring lock
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);
    return entry->data;
  }

  // Transition to PENDING if first thread
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&entry->state, &EMPTY, PENDING)) {
    enqueue_io_request(block_id);
  }

  // Block until I/O completes
  while (atomic_load(&entry->state) == PENDING) {
    entry->waiters++;
    pthread_cond_wait(&entry->cond, &entry->mutex);
    entry->waiters--;
  }

  pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);

  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == ERROR) {
    return NULL; // Propagate error
  }

  return entry->data;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about scalable scheduler design under high concurrency and NUMA-aware memory behavior, reflecting real-world cloud and fintech workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Per-CPU run queues enabling lock-free enqueue/dequeue operations
- Dynamic priority boosting based on wait-time and latency telemetry
- NUMA-aware task placement to minimize memory access latency
- Global I/O-priority bitmap for efficient priority scanning
- Lock-free enqueue using compare-and-swap (CAS) primitives

## Validation Approach
Fairness and throughput under contention can be validated through:
1. **Fairness testing**: Measure wait time distribution across task types using synthetic workloads with controlled I/O and CPU-bound ratios
2. **Throughput benchmarking**: Run high-concurrency workloads and measure tasks completed per second
3. **NUMA locality analysis**: Track remote vs. local memory accesses using hardware counters
4. **Starvation detection**: Verify I/O-bound tasks receive CPU time within bounded latency under sustained CPU contention
5. **Load balance profiling**: Monitor task migration frequency and queue depth across CPUs

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Task {
    atomic_int priority;
    u64 wait_time;
    u64 last_io_latency;
    struct Task *next;
} Task;

typedef struct PerCpuQueue {
    Task *head;
    atomic_T tail;
} PerCpuQueue;

void enqueue(Task *t) {
    PerCpuQueue *q = &per_cpu_queues[smp_processor_id()];
    Task *old_tail = atomic_load(&q->tail);
    do {
        t->next = old_tail;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange(&q->tail, &old_tail, t));
}

Task *dequeue(void) {
    int cpu = find_highest_priority_cpu();
    PerCpuQueue *q = &per_cpu_queues[cpu];
    return atomic_fetch_head(&q->head);
}

void rebalance(void) {
    for_each_cpu(cpu) {
        if (queue_depth(cpu) > threshold)
            migrate_to_neighbor(cpu);
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of process lifecycle, waitpid usage, and zombie avoidance in a practical multi-child scenario.

## Key Concepts
- waitpid semantics and looping
- SIGCHLD handling and reaping
- WNOHANG and non-blocking variants
- Zombie processes and edge cases (stopped/continued)

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

int main() {
  // spawn N children ...
  int finished = 0; int status;
  while (finished < N) {
    pid_t pid = waitpid(-1, &status, 0);
    if (pid > 0) { finished++; /* process status */ }
    else if (pid == -1 && errno == ECHILD) break;
  }
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIGCHLD storms or large N?
- What are the risks of ignoring zombie cleanup in long-running daemons?

